L80: # Durable Object Container API
L81: 
L82: Last updated Sep 30, 2026|Copy as Markdown|cite46†View as Markdown |cite37†Agent setup L83: 
L84: [Select]
L85: 
L86: Each cite1†container is managed and proxied by a Durable Object. The Durable Object manages routing and persistent state. The container process runs your image inside a Linux VM.
L87: 
L88: The API documented on this page is available on `this.ctx.container` inside any Durable Object class that has a container binding. Use it for direct control over the container process.
L89: 
L90: Note
L91: We recommend starting new applications with the Durable Object Container API. It lets you run container workloads alongside the Durable Object's persistent storage, alarms, and request handling. Use `ctx.container` to control when the container starts, receives traffic, and stops, while the Durable Object manages application state and coordination. For existing applications that use the `Container` class, refer to cite12†Migrate to the Durable Object Container API .
L92: Your Durable Object also has access to cite47†SQLite storage through `this.ctx.storage`, cite48†alarms , and all other Durable Object APIs. Use storage to preserve configuration and state across container restarts. Use alarms to schedule work without keeping the container running.
L93: index.js js
L94: 
L95:     `import { DurableObject } from "cloudflare:workers";
L96: 
L97:     export class MyDurableObject extends DurableObject {
L98:     	async start() {
L99:     		const container = this.ctx.container;
L100:     		if (!container) {
L101:     			throw new Error("No container is configured for this Durable Object");
L102:     		}
L103:     		if (!container.running) {
L104:     			// With the `default` scheduling policy, call `container.start()` without options.
L105:     			container.start({
L106:     				image: container.images.base,
L107:     				enableInternet: false,
L108:     			});
L109:     			await this.ctx.storage.put("lastStartedAt", Date.now());
L110:     		}
L111:     	}
L112: 
L113:     	async scheduleStart(timestamp) {
L114:     		await this.ctx.storage.setAlarm(timestamp);
L115:     	}
L116: 
L117:     	async alarm() {
L118:     		await this.start();
L119:     	}
L120:     }`
